The story of Thip

Thip was the fifth son of Mart, a minor lord in charge of the 
Seventy-Second Gnomish Mining Regiment.  His elder brothers all helped 
out at the Regiment, while he enjoyed an idyllic life of being tutored 
and his private explorations.  He knew that someday he'd be a part of 
the proud hustle and bustle, and he looked forward to it, but he also 
enjoyed the time off.

One Friday, after lessons, he headed out to a nearby cave to play with 
his friends.  Industrious dwarves, ever the loners, were tunneling 
nearby.  The moss on the ceiling let off a pearl glow, perfect for a 
rousing game of Rothe-Tag.

They had barely begun, when in the distance they heard a commotion.  A 
crazed, bleeding elder gnome ran by, hurriedly.  He saw them, and 
shouted, "Run, kids!  Hide!  There's a surface dweller on the way!  He's 
crazy!  He invaded our mines, and when we went to ask his intentions, he 
started throwing daggers!  He killed the boss, and then ate his flesh 
off his bones, the savage!"

Hearing this, Thip went into a faint.  It was true.  His father was 
dead, his brothers too.  It seemed like overnight his life was ripped 
from him by that evil surface dweller.  He'd heard stories about them, 
about their 'sun' and their 'sky', but he'd always thought they were 
just that, stories.  He had to resort to skulking around near the 
minetown, eking out an existence from begging.  He heard that surface 
dweller had later met his fate deep in the main branch of the dungeon, 
dying from a falling rock trap.  It didn't seem violent enough, to him, 
to quench the burning thirst for revenge his father's slaughter had 
awoken in him.  But what could he do?  He was a little gnome, barely 
able to take care of himself!

More surface dwellers came, passing through, most of them committing 
atrocities before meeting their own demise.  He was close to starvation 
most of the time, so he barely noticed.  He was shocked when he stumbled 
upon a large corpse while scavenging for food.  It was a surface 
dweller!  From the wounds, it looked like the brute had been on the 
losing side of a pick-axe fight with a dwarven miner.  Some of the old 
anger welled to the surface, and he kicked at the corpse, wanting to 
inflict his own damage.  A lembas wafer dropped out of the traveler's 
sack!  He fell upon it, hungrily, his rage temporarily overridden by his 
more basic hunger.

As he ate, his motions knocked loose a spiked wand from the sack.  He 
examined it, then picked it up as a souvenir.  Suddenly, he heard a 
familiar commotion from the stairway area.  Revenge singing in his 
heart, he rushed towards the sound, against the wave of gnomes running 
away.  Another surface dweller, outlandishly dressed in a leather jacket 
and a hat, was swinging a pick-axe wildly at anything that moved.

Clutching his spiked wand, he walked purposefully towards the mad fiend, 
his vengeance at last in sight.  The brute's eyes met his, and he raised 
the wand and activated it.  The death ray zipped towards the human, then 
whizzed by it.  If it had been wearing any pants, they would have been 
soiled right now, and it was so relieved that it didn't even notice the 
death ray bouncing...

